    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The caves were cut in two phases: a first group of about the 2nd century BCE to 1st century CE, and a second, larger group in the 5th century CE, which Walter Spink dates to the years 460 to 480 and other scholars place over a longer span.</li><li>Caves 1, 2, 16 and 17 form the largest surviving body of ancient Indian wall painting, which mostly tells the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a> of the Buddha's past lives; the later caves were paid for by single patrons seeking merit.</li><li>The site was known to local people and to pilgrims long before 1819, and its murals have since been damaged by neglect, varnish and cleaning, while many 19th-century copies of them were lost in fires.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1819 a British officer scratched his name and the date over a painting of a bodhisattva in Cave 10; because he stood on a five-foot pile of rubble, the inscription now sits well above the eye level of an adult.</p></aside>
    </section>

