    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">2nd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The earliest caves were begun, and the oldest inscription at the site, in Cave 10, is dated by its Brahmi letters to about this time</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 400</span><span class="rf-text">The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> recorded Buddhist pilgrims visiting the site</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 462</span><span class="rf-text">Work on new caves began in many places at once under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena">Harishena</a>'s court, according to Spink</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">477</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena">Harishena</a> died, and major excavation soon ceased except at Cave 26</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1819</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-smith-explorer">John Smith</a>, guided by a local shepherd boy to Cave 10, brought the caves to the attention of the British</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1866</span><span class="rf-text">A fire at the Crystal Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> destroyed all but four of Robert Gill's 27 copies of the murals</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920s</span><span class="rf-text">The last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> had Italian restorers work on the paintings and turned the site into a museum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1983</span><span class="rf-text">The Ajanta Caves were listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ajanta Caves lie in the northern wall of the U-shaped gorge of the River Waghur on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>. They are generally agreed to have been made in two distinct phases, the first during the 2nd century BCE to 1st century CE and the second several centuries later. The earliest group is caves 9, 10, 12, 13 and 15A. According to Walter Spink these were made between 100 BCE and 100 CE, probably under the patronage of the Satavahana dynasty (230 BCE to 220 CE); other datings prefer the period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> (300 to 100 BCE). These first caves were paid for by a number of different patrons seeking merit, and several inscriptions record the donation of particular portions of a single cave.</p><p>The second phase began in the 5th century and is attributed to the theistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Spink argues that the site lay dormant until the mid-5th century, although the early caves stayed in use and the Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> recorded pilgrims there around 400 CE. The later caves were each commissioned as a complete unit by a single patron from among the local rulers or their court elites, again for merit in the Buddhist afterlife, as inscriptions such as those in Cave 17 state.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Ajanta Caves&#8221; cites where it covers History, Caves of the first period (Satavahana), Caves of the later or Vakataka period, Spink's chronology and cave history:</p><ul><li>Walter M. Spink, Ajanta: History and Development, Volume 5: Cave by Cave (Brill, 2007)</li><li>Walter M. Spink, Ajanta: History and Development, Volume 4: Painting, Sculpture, Architecture, Year by Year (Brill, 2009)</li><li>Om Datt Upadhya, The Art of Ajanta and Sopoćani: A Comparative Study: an Enquiry in Prāṇa Aesthetics (Motilal Banarsidass, 1994)</li><li>Pia Brancaccio, The Buddhist Caves at Aurangabad: Transformations in Art and Religion (Brill, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jataka-tales.jpg" alt="Jataka tales" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_India,_Andhra_Pradesh,_possibly_Amaravati,_Satavahana_period_-_Buddha%27s_Previous_Life_as_a_Woodpecker-_Javasakunda_Jataka_-_1970.350_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The caves are 30 rock-cut Buddhist monuments carved into a wall of rock 75 metres long, though 36 foundations have been identified, some added later with letters such as 15A; the numbering is a convenience and does not follow the order of building. Most are viharas, monasteries with a square hall, a cloister-like aisle on each side and small square cells for monks, which in the later period gained a shrine with a large Buddha at the rear. The other main type is the chaitya-griha, a worship hall divided into a nave and two aisles by a row of pillars, with a stupa in the apse that visitors walked around. Caves 9 and 10 are early halls; caves 19 and 26 are later ones, with a Buddha carved on the stupa's front.</p><p>The walls hold paintings and sculpture. The paintings predominantly narrate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a>, legends of the Buddha's previous births as an animal or a human. Cave 1 concentrates on lives in which he was a king, and the two best-known single painted figures are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">bodhisattvas</a> Padmapani and Vajrapani beside its shrine entrance. Cave 2 shows many noble and powerful women, and one of its frescos shows children at a school. Caves 16 and 17 add scenes from the Buddha's life and further Jatakas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The caves are carved from flood basalt and granite of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> Traps. The rock is layered and variable in quality, which forced the carvers to change their plans in places and later caused cracks and collapses, such as the lost portico of Cave 1. Excavation began with a narrow tunnel cut at roof level that was widened downwards and outwards, as the unfinished caves 21 to 24 and the abandoned Cave 28 show. The artists probably carved the rock and made the pillars, roofs and idols themselves, and sculpture and painting inside a cave went on in parallel.</p><p>The murals are in dry fresco, painted on a dry plaster surface rather than into wet plaster. Compositions spread in all directions from a central figure or group instead of running in horizontal bands. In the later caves the painters generally worked on finished areas while excavation continued elsewhere in the cave. Spink's account has the work abandoned in 478 after a brief busy period, which he gives as the reason for unpainted areas such as Cave 4 and the plastered but never painted shrine of Cave 17. Lapis lazuli, which Pia Brancaccio notes must have come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, was used for the figures of foreigners.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Scholars read the paintings as a view of life between the 2nd century BCE and the 5th century CE, showing royalty, the elite, common people, monks and rishis, along with shops, festivals and performance. They contrast the renouncing monk with the sensual life of the materialistic. Many figures in Caves 1, 2 and 17 have foreign faces or dress, which Brancaccio takes as evidence of a prosperous society active in international trade. Spink and other scholars hold that colonial art historians misdated the so-called Persian Embassy Scene in Cave 1 to the 7th century, and read it as an incomplete 5th-century painting of a Jataka tale.</p><p>Nineteenth-century European responses were often critical. William Dalrymple writes that Orientalists lacking knowledge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a> could not understand the subjects and projected their own assumptions onto them, and early Western observers found the luxurious, sensuous paintings out of place in a monastic setting. Richard Cohen, by contrast, writes that to the Indian and Buddhist mind the caves are the religious and the secular fused together. Spink calls the site's rapid transformation between 460 and 480 one of the most remarkable creative achievements in human history.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Ajanta Caves&#8221; cites where it covers Reception history, Foreigners in the paintings of Ajanta:</p><ul><li>Walter M. Spink, Ajanta: History and Development, Volume 5: Cave by Cave (Brill, 2007)</li><li>Om Datt Upadhya, The Art of Ajanta and Sopoćani: A Comparative Study: an Enquiry in Prāṇa Aesthetics (Motilal Banarsidass, 1994)</li><li>Walter M. Spink, Ajanta: History and Development, Volume 4: Painting, Sculpture, Architecture, Year by Year (Brill, 2009)</li><li>Dieter Schlingloff, Ajanta – Handbuch der Malereien (Otto Harrassowitz Verlag, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="Xuanzang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xuanzang_w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The caves were not forgotten. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> recorded pilgrims around 400, the Chinese traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> described them in the 7th century, a Rashtrakuta inscription of the late 7th or early 8th century is the last at the site, and Abu al-Fazl's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari">Ain-i-Akbari</a> mentions twenty-four rock-cut temples. Richard Cohen suggests they stayed in use without a stable Buddhist community. On 28 April 1819 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-smith-missionary">John Smith</a>, a British cavalry officer hunting tigers, was shown the entrance to Cave 10 by a local shepherd boy; the caves were well known to locals already. He had villagers cut back the jungle and scratched his name over a painting.</p><p>Neglect and some restoration damaged the work. In the early 1920s the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>, whose territory included the site, had the paintings restored by two Italian experts and opened a museum and a road, efforts that Cohen says were mismanaged and hastened decay; according to Spink and others, Griffiths's cheap varnish and recent cleaning by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> also harmed the murals. Robert Gill's copies of 1844 to 1863 mostly burned in 1866, and over a hundred of John Griffiths's canvases burned in 1885. A landslide in 1956 led to the excavation of Cave 30, perhaps the oldest cave.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Ajanta Caves&#8221; cites where it covers Colonial era/Rediscovery, Recent excavations:</p><ul><li>Walter M. Spink, Ajanta: History and Development, Volume 5: Cave by Cave (Brill, 2007)</li><li>Om Datt Upadhya, The Art of Ajanta and Sopoćani: A Comparative Study: an Enquiry in Prāṇa Aesthetics (Motilal Banarsidass, 1994)</li><li>Debala Mitra, Ajanta (Archaeological Survey of India, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kizil-caves"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kizil-caves.jpg" alt="Kizil Caves" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kizil-caves">Kizil Caves</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kizil_sky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hiroki Ogawa, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The paintings and the style they come from probably influenced painting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, and some influence has been suggested at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizil-caves">Kizil Caves</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. In the 19th and early 20th centuries a series of copying projects followed: Gill, Griffiths and his students, Christiana Herringham and a group from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> School of Art including Nandalal Bose, and the Japanese artist Arai Kampo, whose tracings perished in the 1923 Great Kanto earthquake. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoria-and-albert-museum">Victoria and Albert Museum</a> still holds 166 surviving Gill and Griffiths paintings.</p><p>The rediscovery gave Indian artists examples from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to follow. Nandalal Bose experimented with ancient techniques in forming his own style, and Abanindranath Tagore and Syed Thajudeen also drew on the paintings. The caves inspired Anna Pavlova's ballet Ajanta's Frescoes, which premiered at Covent Garden in 1923, and the opening poem of Muriel Rukeyser's collection Beast in View (1944). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> is responsible for the site, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed in 1983. The dating of the second phase remains disputed between Spink's brief chronology and a longer one.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ajanta Caves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> are 30 Buddhist rock-cut monasteries and worship halls made in two phases, from about the 2nd century BCE and in the 5th century CE. Their murals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a> are the largest surviving body of ancient Indian wall painting, and the dating of the second phase is still debated.</em></p>

